<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Heathen Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">865–<a href="/years/878">878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Great Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Heathen Army was a large coalition of Scandinavian warriors that invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 865. It took York in 867, overran East Anglia and much of Mercia, and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> defeated it at Edington in 878. Its leaders settled their followers in Northumbria, East Anglia and the east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, creating the region later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army.jpg" alt="Great Heathen Army" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:England_Great_Army_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfred the Great (r. 871–899) held off the <b>Great Heathen Army</b> and fortified Wessex with a network of burhs, and his son Edward the Elder and daughter Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, reconquered the Danelaw south of the Humber in the 910s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The army was broken by Ecgberht of Wessex at the Battle of Ellendun in 825 and could not withstand the <b>Great Heathen Army</b>, which occupied Repton in 873–874.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century Northumbria faced the Vikings, and in 867 its rival kings Osberht and Ælla were killed trying to retake York from the <b>Great Heathen Army</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Heathen Army</b> that landed in 865 conquered Northumbria, East Anglia and much of Mercia, and attacked Wessex in 871, the ‘year of battles’, which included the West Saxon victory at Ashdown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In England the <b>Great Heathen Army</b> landed in 865, took York, killed King Edmund of East Anglia in 869 and overran Northumbria, East Anglia and much of Mercia, until Alfred the Great of the Kingdom of Wessex defeated Guthrum at Edington in 878.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>At Lüneburg Heath, in today's Lower Saxony, the Norse <b>Great Heathen Army</b> defeated the army of King Louis the Younger.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
